body:
`data` is assigned via `boundData || ...`, but `useDataScope(schema.bind)` returns the whole `dataSource` when `schema.bind` is undefined. With a truthy object `dataSource`, this can incorrectly override `schema.data/schema.props.data` and trigger the “Table data must be an array” error even when inline data is valid. Consider only using `boundData` when it’s an array (or when `schema.bind` is provided), and keep the fallback order for inline data.
```suggestion
  // Try to get data from binding first (when provided), then fall back to inline data
  const hasBind = schema?.bind !== undefined && schema.bind !== null && schema.bind !== '';
  const boundData = hasBind ? useDataScope(schema.bind) : undefined;
  const data = hasBind ? boundData : (schema.data ?? schema.props?.data ?? []);
```

The `columns` initializer is a nested ternary, which is hard to read and extend. Consider extracting `rawColumns` (from `schema.columns` / `schema.props?.columns`) and then applying a single `Array.isArray` guard, matching the clearer pattern used in the list/tree-view changes.
```suggestion
  const rawColumns = schema.columns ?? schema.props?.columns;
  const columns = Array.isArray(rawColumns) ? rawColumns : [];
```
